(Batch B, docs/ledger/expressions.md EXP-18, EXP-29, EXP-30, EXP-35,
 EXP-39, EXP-41, EXP-43, EXP-73, EXP-83, EXP-84. Kinds 360-366, the
 first seven slots of this file's second reserved span (360-369; 367-369
 are spare - ten raw remap slots, seven leaves). Same discipline as
 batch A throughout: drawn only at depth 3, quoted multi-word companion
 names off 'gen buffer name'/'gen buffer companion', a value asserted
 against a compound expression is always captured into its own named
 variable first, extreme literals wherever the magnitude is genuinely
 free (integer context: the full i64 span; float context: the EXP-94
 ruling's 15-digit whole-part cap, or narrower where hand-verification
 found a construct-specific reason to go narrower - see the cast leaf's
 own comment). Priority followed CLAUDE.md's own ordering, not row
 order: EXP-73 (a buffer-backed text staying independent of the buffer
 that made it) and EXP-83/84 (the base-conversion paths no existing
 leaf reaches) are the memory-safety- and gap-value-flagged rows the
 ledger's own Report section names; the cast-precedence and predicate
 rows round out an even ten without chasing arithmetic for its own
 sake, which CLAUDE.md is explicit is "nearly worthless" on its own.)

(A restricted draw off the SAME alphabet 'gen leaf character literal'
 already declares (gen_expr_safe_chars) - positions 1-62 only, which by
 that list's own fixed ordering (A-Z, a-z, 0-9, then punctuation) is
 letters and digits alone. Buffer content built from this can be
 embedded straight into a double-quoted string in an EMITTED program
 with no escaping question at all - the punctuation tail of that same
 list holds `{{`/`}}`, which are exactly the two characters a plain
 interpolated embed would need to double first, so this leaf sidesteps
 that entirely rather than doubling anything itself.)

To 'gen expr safe text' with a number called 'how many'.
    a text called built is "".
    a number called i is 0.
    While i is less than 'how many',
        a number called 'the position' is 'rng below' of 62 add 1,
        a text called 'the char' is element 'the position' of gen_expr_safe_chars,
        Set built to "{built}{'the char'}",
        Set i to i add 1.
    Return a text, built.

(A digit-value LIST evaluated positionally - value = ((d1 * base) + d2)
 * base + d3 ... - the same rule EXP-83/84's own parser follows, so the
 leaf below computes its expected value by the identical accumulation
 rather than a separate formula that could drift from the parser's own.)

To 'gen expr base value' with a number called base and a list called digits.
    a number called value is 0.
    a number called i is 0.
    a number called 'how many' is digits's length.
    While i is less than 'how many',
        a number called 'the slot' is i add 1,
        a number called d is element 'the slot' of digits,
        Set value to value multiply base add d,
        Set i to i add 1.
    Return a number, value.

(EXP-18: `the number` - the documented spelling of the `for each` loop
 iterator (LANGUAGE.md:1932) - referenced inside the loop body and
 summed across every iteration, checked against the same sum computed
 by walking 1..bound in the GENERATOR itself rather than a closed-form
 N*(N+1)/2, which would need parenthesised grouping to keep the
 multiply and divide in the right order - and parentheses are Vox
 comments, not grouping, in this very generator's own source (this
 ledger's own Discrepancy 5). `gen_flow.vox`'s loop leaves only ever
 emit the bare `number` spelling (EXP-19, already exercised); this is
 the only leaf that writes the article in front of it. The bound is a
 loop COUNT, a structural shape choice like EXP-51's subject count -
 LANGUAGE.md pins no limit on it either way, so it is a plain
 'rng below' draw, not an extreme-number magnitude. One line, top level
 only, the loop-leaf discipline PROCEDURE.md 6a spells out.)

To 'gen leaf article the number iterator' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'the bound draw' is 'rng below' of 30.
    a number called 'the bound' is 'the bound draw' add 1.
    a number called 'the expected total' is 0.
    a number called 'the tally position' is 1.
    While 'the tally position' is less than 'the bound' add 1,
        Set 'the expected total' to 'the expected total' add 'the tally position',
        Set 'the tally position' to 'the tally position' add 1.
    'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the total name' is 'gen buffer companion' of "running total".
    a text called 'the total decl' is "a number called {'the total name'} is 0".
    a text called 'the loop line' is "For each number from 1 to {'the bound'}, Set {'the total name'} to {'the total name'} add the number".
    a text called 'the check' is 'gen assert number is' of "EXP-18" and 'the total name' and 'the expected total'.
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the total decl', 'the loop line', 'the check'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(EXP-29, EXP-30: a cast binds tighter than arithmetic and applies only
 to the expression immediately to its left (EXP-29), and bracing the
 whole expression before the cast overrides that (EXP-30) -
 LANGUAGE.md:1950, 2174. The tight half is demonstrated the way EXP-11
 already proves it has to work: a TEXT operand next to `add` only
 compiles at all under tight binding, since a loose reading would put
 the text directly in arithmetic, a compile error (this ledger's own
 EXP-11/EXP-12/EXP-13) - so a program that compiles and gives the right
 sum is doubly strong evidence. The braced half needs the two readings
 to genuinely differ to mean anything, so it uses two FLOAT operands
 whose fractional parts can carry a truncation past a whole-number
 boundary, the same shape the ledger's own probe uses (2.5/3.5 -> 5.5
 tight vs 6 braced).

 RUTHLESS REWORK: the text-cast half draws both operands from the full
 extreme span (integer context, i64 wrap-consistent, same reasoning as
 every other leaf in this file). The braced half is NOT drawn from the
 full extreme span - two SUMMED 15-digit-whole floats (EXP-94's own cap)
 were hand-verified to drift the truncated total by a real unit in the
 last place (`123456789012345.49 add 111111111111111.50` truncates one
 low: 234567900123456 expected, 234567900123457 actual on both
 compilers) - a genuine cliff, but a DIFFERENT one than EXP-94's own
 (that construct adds an exact 0.5 to ONE imprecise float; this one
 sums TWO of them), so dragging it into this row would rediscover
 EXP-94's finding under a different ID instead of testing what EXP-30
 actually claims. Capped at 12 whole digits a side instead -
 hand-verified exact through the fraction-carry boundary both directions
 at that cap on both compilers - which is what the "FLOAT-context
 values stay <=15 digits" ruling means for a construct that sums two of
 them rather than adding to one.)

To 'gen leaf cast tight and braced' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a number called 'the base number' is 'gen expr extreme value'.
    a number called 'the addend' is 'gen expr extreme value'.
    a number called 'the tight expected' is 'the base number' add 'the addend'.
    'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the digits name' is 'gen buffer companion' of "digit text".
    a text called 'the digits decl' is "a text called {'the digits name'} is \"{'the base number'}\"".
    'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the tight result name' is 'gen buffer companion' of "tight cast result".
    a text called 'the tight decl' is "a number called {'the tight result name'} is {'the digits name'} as a number add {'the addend'}".
    a text called 'the tight check' is 'gen assert number is' of "EXP-29" and 'the tight result name' and 'the tight expected'.
    a number called 'the first whole length' is 'rng below' of 12 add 1.
    a text called 'the first whole text' is 'gen random digits' of 'the first whole length'.
    a number called 'the first whole' is 'the first whole text' as a number.
    a number called 'the first frac' is 'rng below' of 100.
    'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the first float name' is 'gen buffer companion' of "first braced reading".
    a text called 'the first float decl' is "a float called {'the first float name'} is {'the first whole'}.{'the first frac':02}".
    a number called 'the second whole length' is 'rng below' of 12 add 1.
    a text called 'the second whole text' is 'gen random digits' of 'the second whole length'.
    a number called 'the second whole' is 'the second whole text' as a number.
    a number called 'the second frac' is 'rng below' of 100.
    'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'the second float name' is 'gen buffer companion' of "second braced reading".
    a text called 'the second float decl' is "a float called {'the second float name'} is {'the second whole'}.{'the second frac':02}".
    a number called 'the frac sum' is 'the first frac' add 'the second frac'.
    a number called 'the braced expected' is 'the first whole' add 'the second whole'.
    If 'the frac sum' is greater than 99 then,
